        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-07-13T16:54:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "thank you for well documented code!\n\nWhy can\u0027t we have both, monitoring Vbus and monitor CC lines? I believe both changes are called out in the spec for changing state to disconnect",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 650,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 651,
        "endChar": 55
      },
      "revId": "d5e0b0903d9899031d72c9b5f220e1fc6214c381",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dda65d21_bf19dd61",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/it83xx.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 651,
      "author": {
        "id": 1288462
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-07-17T09:03:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Because USB-TypeC spec defines that exiting from attached.SNK to unattached.SNK is by monitoring Vbus (see 4.5.2.2.5.2 exiting from attached.SNK state), but it doesn\u0027t refer to by monitoring CC lines.\n\nI think this is reasonable, because if we are SNK and monitoring CC lines ~0v for disconnection, any messages on cc lines may falls ~0v then the a few interrupt fire. And this is why I disable the interrupt in TCPM when we\u0027re SNK role.\n\nIf we\u0027re source, following the spec, we should monitoring CC lines ~3.3v for disconnection. All the messages on cc lines doesn\u0027t raise to ~3.3v, so the disconnection event can be always correctly triggered.",
      "parentUuid": "5591d7fc_f4f7cc1a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 650,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 651,
        "endChar": 55
      },
      "revId": "d5e0b0903d9899031d72c9b5f220e1fc6214c381",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a6100469_bb2a956e",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/it83xx.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 651,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-07-20T15:59:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Because USB-TypeC spec defines that exiting from attached.SNK to unattached.SNK is by monitoring Vbus (see 4.5.2.2.5.2 exiting from attached.SNK state), but it doesn\u0027t refer to by monitoring CC lines.\n\u003e \n\u003e I think this is reasonable, because if we are SNK and monitoring CC lines ~0v for disconnection, any messages on cc lines may falls ~0v then the a few interrupt fire. And this is why I disable the interrupt in TCPM when we\u0027re SNK role.\n\u003e \n\u003e If we\u0027re source, following the spec, we should monitoring CC lines ~3.3v for disconnection. All the messages on cc lines doesn\u0027t raise to ~3.3v, so the disconnection event can be always correctly triggered.\n\nSection \"4.5.2.2.5.2  Exiting from Attached.SNK State\" also says:\n\n\"A port that is monitoring the CC voltage for disconnect (but is not in the process of a USB PD PR_Swap or USB PD FR_Swap) shall transition to Unattached.SNK within tSinkDisconnect after the CC voltage remains below vRd-USB for tPDDebounce.\"\n\nSo we should monitor both Vbus and CC lines when we are the SNK. We do need to debounce the CC lines for tPDDDebounce (10 - 20 ms) otherwise we would get false positives for PD communication like you pointed out.",
      "parentUuid": "dda65d21_bf19dd61",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 650,
        "startChar": 5,
        "endLine": 651,
        "endChar": 55
      },
